WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:03.712
There's quite a lot of difference,
but also a lot of similarities

00:00:03.712 --> 00:00:06.047
between an architect
and an interior designer.

00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:10.802
And today, I want to talk about
how each profession develops the concept.

00:00:10.885 --> 00:00:15.515
And I wanted to talk about the concept
because it's really the foundation

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:20.562
of every design project and it's something
that informs every aspect of the design.

00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:26.026
And fundamentally, it's how we approach
problems and come up with solutions.

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:29.946
I'll be talking about the difference
in how we approach problems.

00:00:29.946 --> 00:00:33.450
I'll also be talking
about the hierarchy of importance

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.787
for each profession and how this can lead
to very different results.

00:00:37.912 --> 00:00:40.540
A portion of this video is sponsored
by Sound Corps.

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:42.792
More on them later in this video.

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:47.005
Of course,
this is based on my personal observations.

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:49.716
I am an architect,
trained as an architect.

00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:53.887
And I went through the traditional process
of becoming an architect.

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:58.808
But I did work in an interior design
slash architecture firm

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:03.271
where I was the only architect working
alongside other interior designers.

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:10.153
And this process was so interesting to me
and so eye opening.

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:15.492
And I actually saw a lot of flaws in my
own training and how I approach design.

00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:20.163
So if you're an interior designer
watching this, please feel free to share

00:01:20.163 --> 00:01:23.958
your thoughts and experiences
so that this comment section can become

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:25.376
a source of information

00:01:25.376 --> 00:01:28.671
for young people who are trying to decide
which profession to go into.

00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:30.215
I also have a discourse server

00:01:30.215 --> 00:01:34.385
where you can ask questions
and we have a pretty big variety of people

00:01:34.385 --> 00:01:38.556
from different professions and backgrounds
and experiences and skills.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.393
So yeah, if you have any questions,
feel free to drop it in

00:01:42.393 --> 00:01:45.105
there as well.

00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:49.692
So high level architects
design the building and interior designers

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:54.697
design within that shell,
but there's also a lot of in-betweens

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:58.118
and a lot of architects
do interior renovations.

00:01:58.118 --> 00:02:02.288
And personally I've worked on
a lot of interior renovations in my career

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:07.210
and really every project is unique
based on the client and the project type.

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:09.754
So keep that in mind
as you're watching this.

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:13.633
And if you want a full breakdown
of the differences

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.847
like of the project types
and the design process and the education,

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:23.226
as well as the skills and personality
that's required to thrive in each career.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.186
Check out the video right here.

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:29.023
I think there's a lot of nuances
to the way we approach problems.

00:02:29.023 --> 00:02:30.775
That's quite different

00:02:32.944 --> 00:02:34.112
when it came to developing

00:02:34.112 --> 00:02:37.657
the concept, and I really try
to distill it down to the essence.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:41.828
I think the biggest difference
is that architects

00:02:42.036 --> 00:02:45.915
derive their concepts
from external sources and

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:49.544
interior designers derive their sources
from internal sources.

00:02:49.961 --> 00:02:50.837
Let me explain.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:54.215
A concept is an idea
that becomes a foundation

00:02:54.215 --> 00:02:57.927
and identity of every design project,
and it drives a forward.

00:02:58.052 --> 00:03:01.806
It's the idea that informs
the different aspects of your building.

00:03:02.098 --> 00:03:07.145
In every step of the design process
and whenever you're stuck,

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:12.150
you can refer back to the concept
to get some clarity in your direction.

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:15.486
It's also a really good way of embedding
meaning into the project

00:03:15.486 --> 00:03:18.865
that's beyond just the building
or the space itself.

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:23.411
But most importantly,
it's a tool to get buy in from clients

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:27.415
and your team
and get everyone aligned on the same page.

00:03:27.582 --> 00:03:33.379
Each office and each designer is unique
in their way of developing a concept.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:35.924
So try to learn from others.

00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:40.094
And as you work in different offices,
try to also develop

00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:47.227
your own methods.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:50.230
Architects tend

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.566
to derive their concepts
from external sources.

00:03:53.608 --> 00:03:55.944
When you see an architect
presenting their work,

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:59.781
we typically start off
by describing the context

00:03:59.781 --> 00:04:04.285
or the problems or opportunities
they see on the site or the program.

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:09.540
And this is all of your physical
or social constraints of your project.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:14.170
And when you actually look at the most
convincing presenters like Bjarke Ingels,

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:18.049
they distill the problem
into a really clear series

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:23.680
of diagrams or images, and they use this
to develop their functional

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:28.685
or formal or material strategy,
which forms the basis for their concept.

00:04:28.685 --> 00:04:32.814
But this isn't the case with just large
buildings, even one architecture

00:04:32.814 --> 00:04:36.985
operating at a much smaller scale,
like interior renovations.

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:42.282
The way we present concepts
is almost always dependent

00:04:42.282 --> 00:04:47.370
on an external or site specific
or program specific source.

