00:06:14.624 --> 00:06:16.334
I'll give you an example with a project

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:19.212
that I'm working on right now,
which is a library renovation,

00:06:19.587 --> 00:06:24.133
where the driving concept
is this idea of the urban courtyard.

00:06:24.258 --> 00:06:28.179
This was from a series
of contextual factors.

00:06:28.513 --> 00:06:34.519
The library has this really beautiful
garden that already kind of functions

00:06:34.519 --> 00:06:38.815
as a central greenspace
in this very dense neighborhood.

00:06:38.981 --> 00:06:43.778
And you can see this solid void
relationship within that building as well.

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:48.908
When you enter the building,
you've got this big three storey atrium

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:52.537
and the library is also
in a really diverse neighborhood.

00:06:52.703 --> 00:06:56.624
It's got a really high percentage
of immigrants

00:06:56.624 --> 00:06:59.836
and people
whose first language is not English.

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:04.507
The functional program of the library
is also pretty diverse.

00:07:04.507 --> 00:07:09.011
We're putting in a digital media lab,
a lounge, a recording studio,

00:07:09.011 --> 00:07:10.555
a grab and go section.

00:07:10.555 --> 00:07:22.024
So we developed this idea of the urban
courtyard, and courtyards

00:07:22.024 --> 00:07:26.863
have been pretty universal across
different cultures throughout history.

00:07:26.863 --> 00:07:32.326
And although it first came to place
to control the micro climactic conditions

00:07:32.326 --> 00:07:37.623
within buildings, over time it evolved
to become a social gathering place.

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:42.420
And so this shared architectural language
of the courtyard brings these diverse

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:47.675
cultures and programs together while
also bringing in an element of nature.

00:07:47.884 --> 00:07:50.344
You can see how we developed the concept

00:07:50.344 --> 00:07:54.223
by tying it
to some of these external conditions.

00:07:54.223 --> 00:07:59.312
The broader and more flexible your concept
is, the better, because you can tie it

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:03.232
to a lot of different aspects
of the design, for example, the courtyard.

00:08:03.357 --> 00:08:06.402
This can be functional or formal or social

00:08:06.402 --> 00:08:10.615
or organizational,
and it has a lot of visual associations.

00:08:10.615 --> 00:08:13.576
So it can be also used to develop
your material strategy.

00:08:13.576 --> 00:08:16.245
But a concept like, let's say

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:20.666
a digital hub, which is an idea
that we've been talking a lot about.

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:25.213
It doesn't have a lot of historical
or architectural or visual

00:08:25.213 --> 00:08:29.175
associations, so it's a little bit harder
to use it as a concept.

00:08:29.258 --> 00:08:33.095
Even the most form driven architecture
like Frank Gehry's

00:08:33.095 --> 00:08:36.432
work is in some way
relating to its context.

00:08:36.432 --> 00:08:39.727
Now architects will take this,
and some architects

00:08:39.727 --> 00:08:44.482
will create a mood board,
which I'll go into in a little bit.

00:08:44.524 --> 00:08:49.195
But when it comes to the hierarchy
of importance for architects,

00:08:49.195 --> 00:08:54.742
like based on our training
and our design philosophy, it's function,

00:08:55.117 --> 00:08:59.413
organization, order, and then we have mood

00:09:01.749 --> 00:09:03.626
for interior designers.

00:09:03.626 --> 00:09:07.838
They don't really have these complex
conditions to work with, right?

00:09:07.922 --> 00:09:12.635
So they derive a lot of their concept
ideas from internal sources.

00:09:12.718 --> 00:09:16.722
And what I found
is that the concept development process

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:20.893
in interior
design is a lot more intimately

00:09:20.893 --> 00:09:24.522
connected to the client
and their visions for the project.

00:09:24.647 --> 00:09:29.068
And so during this phase,
we just sit with the client and we just

00:09:29.068 --> 00:09:33.948
talk about their vision for the space,
not just programmatically or functionally,

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.118
but how they want the space to feel

00:09:37.451 --> 00:09:40.496
and what's the image they want to project.

00:09:40.538 --> 00:09:44.375
And the client, they can bring
their own inspiration images,

00:09:44.709 --> 00:09:48.379
and if it's like a commercial client,
they can bring their own

00:09:48.379 --> 00:09:49.755
brand and guidelines.

00:09:49.755 --> 00:09:54.135
And we basically work with the client
to put together

00:09:54.135 --> 00:09:59.181
visual representation of this feeling,
which is called a mood board.

00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:04.312
But before doing this,
we would write a very small paragraph

00:10:04.312 --> 00:10:08.024
or like a list of words or a poem

00:10:08.107 --> 00:10:13.029
that kind of captures the mood or
the direction that we got from the client.

00:10:13.029 --> 00:10:16.824
And I found this process
super interesting for me.

00:10:16.949 --> 00:10:23.247
Language is not really my strong suit, so
this was definitely kind of hard for me.

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:27.501
But first of all, like, it's
a really, really great way to consolidate

00:10:27.501 --> 00:10:29.587
all the information
that you got from the client.

00:10:29.587 --> 00:10:33.341
But you're not just reciting
what the client is saying.

00:10:33.341 --> 00:10:37.928
You're adding and you're elevating
all the things that they said

00:10:38.304 --> 00:10:42.516
into a language
that have a visual connection.

00:10:42.683 --> 00:10:45.978
So, for example,
we were designing an office

00:10:45.978 --> 00:10:49.649
for a construction company,
and some of the words that they used

00:10:49.857 --> 00:10:54.695
was things like trustworthy,
kind, family business.

00:10:54.695 --> 00:10:59.742
And so you could use that to say something
like Grounded in family roots.

00:10:59.992 --> 00:11:02.244
And this kind of descriptive writing.

00:11:02.286 --> 00:11:07.750
It works hand-in-hand with the Moodboard,
which is basically a collection of images

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:11.295
that represent the mood or the feeling
that you're trying to create.

00:11:11.462 --> 00:11:15.675
These images
aren't necessarily precedent images.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:20.554
They're literally like images
that combine together to create a mood.

00:11:20.596 --> 00:11:22.014
Everyone probably has

00:11:22.014 --> 00:11:26.185
a slightly different way of doing it,
but the way that I was taught,

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:30.856
you shouldn't actually include
images of actual building elements.

00:11:30.898 --> 00:11:34.276
That actually comes in a little bit later
in the design process.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:38.364
I've seen a lot of different reactions
to this concept development process.

00:11:38.656 --> 00:11:43.077
Some clients are a little bit
more literal and more pragmatic,

00:11:43.077 --> 00:11:46.872
and they just want to quickly
move on to the actual design.

00:11:47.331 --> 00:11:50.710
But for a lot of clients,
they get really involved

00:11:50.835 --> 00:11:53.754
and they want to bring their own images.

00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:57.550
And because interior design
is so much more based

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.761
on more elusive things
compared to architecture,

00:12:01.178 --> 00:12:03.848
like they're more focused
on the mood of the space.

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:07.977
I think there's a much higher chance
of misalignment.

00:12:08.269 --> 00:12:12.273
And so I think the step is a really,
really good check point.

00:12:12.273 --> 00:12:14.108
And I think it's really essential

00:12:14.108 --> 00:12:17.236
for everyone just to make sure
that we're all on the same page.

00:12:17.445 --> 00:12:20.322
Once you have the mood board,
you pull together the colors

00:12:20.865 --> 00:12:23.701
which become the basis
for your material board.

00:12:23.743 --> 00:12:27.455
Each profession is really unique,
and each designer

00:12:27.455 --> 00:12:30.833
and each office is really distinct
in how they operate.

00:12:30.875 --> 00:12:35.629
As an architect, I learned so much
from that time when I was working with

00:12:35.629 --> 00:12:39.884
interior designers,
and I think one of the most eye opening

00:12:39.884 --> 00:12:44.930
things was the relationships
that they were building with the clients.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:47.975
And maybe it's a result

00:12:47.975 --> 00:12:51.937
of this more intimate process
where you talk

00:12:51.937 --> 00:12:57.234
for prolonged periods of time
about the client's desires.

00:12:57.234 --> 00:13:02.072
But I found that interior designers
in most cases

00:13:02.323 --> 00:13:06.494
had a much more personal relationship
with their clients.

00:13:06.494 --> 00:13:09.330
And I just thought
it was a really important element

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.832
that I could take into my work
as an architect.

00:13:11.999 --> 00:13:14.835
I hope this video gave you
a little bit of insight

00:13:14.835 --> 00:13:18.839
into the differences
and the nuances of each profession.

00:13:18.881 --> 00:13:20.174
If you have any questions

00:13:20.174 --> 00:13:23.177
or thoughts on the topic,
please let me know in the comments.

00:13:23.177 --> 00:13:26.472
And if you guys found this video
hopeful, make sure to give me a thumbs up

00:13:27.014 --> 00:13:49.286
and I will see you guys in the next video.

